Title: "Selected Open Problems"
Id: "8ac6237e-b34e-4667-8a7a-1fd9950c7476"


Text: "A mathematical theory is defined as much by the questions it cannot yet
       answer as by the theorems it has successfully established. A conjecture
       belongs in a foundational library only when its statement is
       mathematically precise and its unproved status is unmistakable. The open
       problems presented here are not isolated curiosities or recreational
       puzzles; each arises organically from the foundational structures
       developed throughout this volume, and each sits at the epicenter of
       modern mathematical research.

       These problems dramatically illustrate the insurmountable chasm
       separating empirical verification from deductive proof. An assertion
       about all integers or all complex zeros cannot be demonstrated by testing
       billions or trillions of cases. The history of number theory is filled
       with plausible conjectures supported by vast numerical evidence that
       ultimately proved false—such as the Mertens conjecture, which was
       verified for billions of values before being disproved by Andrew Odlyzko
       and Herman te Riele in 1985, or the oscillation of
       $\pi(x) - \operatorname{li}(x)$, which first changes sign only beyond
       Skewes' astronomical numbers.

       In an axiomatic library like **Mathlore**, conjecture cards serve a vital
       architectural role: they state exact mathematical claims without
       compromising deductive integrity, demarcating the precise frontier
       between established truth and open exploration."
Id: "c929b171-e961-4640-a453-7e459851f114"


SectionTitle: "Additive and Multiplicative Structure of Primes"
Id: "67c5e464-3e6e-40d6-9da8-2be12e536dc9"


[\(prime)::integer]
Refines: p
satisfies:
. \integer.one \:integer.less?:/ p
. forAll: d is \integer
  where:
  . \integer.zero \:integer.less?:/ d
  . d \.integer.divides./ p
  then:
  . anyOf:
    . d = \integer.one
    . d = p
Documented:
. adjective: "prime"
. description: "A prime is an integer greater than one whose only positive
                divisors are one and itself."
Id: "133119f5-9771-4e7e-b84e-de640884b36e"


[\integer.two]
Defines: t := \integer.one \.integer.+./ \integer.one is \integer
Documented:
. called: "integer two"
. written: "2"
Id: "4574a4f5-93f4-4a1b-ab6c-31750b94003a"


[\(even)::integer]
Refines: n
satisfies:
. exists: k is \integer
  suchThat: n = \integer.two \.integer.*./ k
Documented:
. adjective: "even"
Id: "90de84c3-1ed2-47f9-913e-1892c1976b5e"


Conjecture:
then:
. forAll: n is \integer
  where:
  . \integer.two \:integer.less?:/ n
  . n is? \(even)::integer
  then:
  . exists: p, q is \integer
    suchThat:
    . p is? \(prime)::integer
    . q is? \(prime)::integer
    . n = p \.integer.+./ q
Documented:
. called: "Strong Goldbach Conjecture"
. description: "Every even integer greater than two is the sum of two primes;
                the two primes are not required to be distinct."
Id: "1945a57b-f397-4f58-b515-fd8878910f2e"


Conjecture:
then:
. forAll: N is \integer
  then:
  . exists: p, q is \integer
    suchThat:
    . N \:integer.less?:/ p
    . p is? \(prime)::integer
    . q = p \.integer.+./ \integer.two
    . q is? \(prime)::integer
Documented:
. called: "Twin Prime Conjecture"
. description: "Above every integer bound there is a prime $p$ for which $p+2$
                is also prime. This unboundedness formulation is equivalent to
                the existence of infinitely many twin-prime pairs."
Id: "565f765c-47cd-427f-a5b5-44c87d1a9794"


Text: "The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic guarantees that the ring of
       integers $\mathbb{Z}$ is a unique factorization domain: every integer
       greater than one factors uniquely into a product of prime numbers. While
       this completely classifies the *multiplicative* structure of the
       integers, the interplay between multiplication and addition remains
       notoriously intractable. Primes are defined multiplicatively, yet both
       Goldbach's conjecture and the Twin Prime conjecture pose questions about
       additive patterns among primes.

       **Historical Milestones on Goldbach's Problem**: First formulated in a
       1742 letter from Christian Goldbach to Leonhard Euler, the conjecture has
       resisted all attempts at complete resolution. In 1937, Ivan Vinogradov
       employed the Hardy--Littlewood circle method to prove the *weak Goldbach
       conjecture* for all sufficiently large odd numbers (every large odd
       integer is the sum of three primes). In 2013, Harald Helfgott finally
       closed all remaining finite bounds, unconditionally proving the weak
       conjecture for *all* odd integers $n \ge 7$. The strong (binary)
       conjecture, however, remains open.

       **Progress on Bounded Gaps Between Primes**: For centuries, Euclid's
       proof of the infinitude of primes offered no control over the gap
       $p_{n+1} - p_n$. In 2013, Yitang Zhang achieved a historic breakthrough
       by proving the existence of an integer $H < 7 \times 10^7$ such that
       infinitely many pairs of consecutive primes satisfy
       $p_{n+1} - p_n \le H$. Through subsequent refinements by James Maynard,
       Terence Tao, and the collaborative Polymath8 project, the bound was
       reduced to $H = 246$ (and $H \le 6$ assuming the Generalized
       Elliott--Halberstam Conjecture). Yet the final step from $H = 246$ to the
       Twin Prime conjecture ($H = 2$) is obstructed by the famous 'parity
       barrier' of sieve theory, which cannot distinguish numbers with an odd
       number of prime factors from those with an even number."
Id: "68b44b77-3844-4b61-9feb-7d441e727db2"


SectionTitle: "Zeros of the Zeta Function"
Id: "1ce51384-d4ed-4ff8-ae1f-1a249702737c"


TextDefinition: "The Riemann Zeta Function and Euler's Golden Key.
                 
                 For complex numbers $s$ with $\operatorname{Re}(s) > 1$, the *Riemann zeta function* is defined
                 by the Dirichlet series:
                 $$ \zeta(s) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^s}. $$
                 In 1737, Leonhard Euler discovered the fundamental identity connecting the zeta function to the
                 prime numbers—the *Euler product formula*:
                 $$ \zeta(s) = \prod_{p \text{ prime}} \frac{1}{1 - p^{-s}}. $$
                 In his landmark 1859 memoir, Bernhard Riemann demonstrated that $\zeta(s)$ extends meromorphically
                 to the entire complex plane $\mathbb{C}$, possessing a single simple pole at $s = 1$ with residue $1$.
                 Riemann proved that $\zeta(s)$ satisfies the majestic functional equation:
                 $$ \xi(s) := \frac{1}{2} s(s-1) \pi^{-s/2} \Gamma\left(\frac{s}{2}\right) \zeta(s) = \xi(1 - s), $$
                 where $\Gamma(s)$ is Euler's gamma function.
                 
                 The zeros of $\zeta(s)$ fall into two distinct families:
                 1. **Trivial Zeros**: The points $s = -2, -4, -6, \ldots$ at the negative even integers, arising
                    from the poles of the gamma function $\Gamma(s/2)$.
                 2. **Nontrivial Zeros**: Infinitely many zeros lying within the *critical strip* $0 < \operatorname{Re}(s) < 1$,
                    distributed symmetrically about the real axis and the *critical line* $\operatorname{Re}(s) = 1/2$."
Id: "6afb5d40-7bb1-43d7-b2e1-c4d5691fe031"


TextConjecture: "The Riemann Hypothesis.
                 
                 Every nontrivial zero $\rho$ of the Riemann zeta function satisfies:
                 $$ \operatorname{Re}(\rho) = \frac{1}{2}. $$
                 
                 That is, all non-trivial zeros lie precisely on the critical vertical line $\operatorname{Re}(s) = 1/2$.
                 
                 *Significance for Prime Number Distribution*:
                 The Prime Number Theorem—proved independently by Jacques Hadamard and Charles Jean de la Vallée Poussin
                 in 1896—is equivalent to the non-vanishing of $\zeta(s)$ on the boundary line $\operatorname{Re}(s) = 1$:
                 $$ \pi(x) \sim \operatorname{li}(x) = \int_2^x \frac{dt}{\ln t}. $$
                 In 1901, Helge von Koch proved that the Riemann Hypothesis is mathematically equivalent to the
                 sharpest possible square-root error bound in the Prime Number Theorem:
                 $$ \pi(x) = \operatorname{li}(x) + O(\sqrt{x} \ln x). $$
                 In this sense, the Riemann Hypothesis asserts that the primes are distributed as uniformly and
                 randomly as quantum noise around their smooth logarithmic integral trend.
                 
                 *Deep Connections across Mathematics*:
                 The spacing of the zeros on the critical line exhibits statistical correlations identical to
                 the eigenvalues of random Hermitian matrices in the Gaussian Unitary Ensemble (Montgomery's Pair
                 Correlation Conjecture and Freeman Dyson, 1972). This striking connection suggests the *Hilbert--Pólya
                 conjecture*: that the imaginary parts of the nontrivial zeros correspond to the eigenvalues of a
                 self-adjoint Hamiltonian operator in quantum mechanics."
Id: "2315cd43-c1d4-4518-bb73-1e2aa34b2df0"


Text: "These conjectures share a profound methodological and epistemological
       lesson. In every era of mathematical development, definitions and proven
       theorems establish the boundary of what is known with absolute certainty.
       Computation and numerical experiments uncover patterns and test
       hypotheses at scale; heuristic models from physics and probability
       suggest why a proposition ought to be true; but only rigorous deductive
       proof can promote a conjecture to an enduring theorem.

       Should any of these celebrated problems be resolved in the future, the
       formal representation in an axiomatic library like **Mathlore** does not
       change its mathematical statement; rather, its epistemological status
       transitions from `Conjecture:` to `Theorem:`, accompanied by a newly
       verified proof that traces every logical deduction back to the
       foundational axioms of logic and set theory."
Id: "3d6d34ce-b17e-42c2-9293-d605cb268841"
